WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.425 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Imagine two identical digits applying for
different jobs in a giant number.

00:00:05.545 --> 00:00:08.780 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One earns ten times as much as its neighbour!

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.593 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
How can the very same digit have such different
values?

00:00:12.713 --> 00:00:15.537 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Let’s uncover its place-value secret.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:21.325 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Today, we’re learning to explain how a digit’s
place changes its value in a whole number.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.334 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You’ll be able to name a digit’s place and give
its value.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.727 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You’ll be able to use a place-value chart to
explain a number.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:36.287 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You’ll be able to explain why the same digit is
worth ten times as much one place to the left.

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:40.170 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
What makes the same digit worth ten times as
much?

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.025 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Its position does.

00:00:42.145 --> 00:00:47.301 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
In our base ten system, ten ones make one ten,
and ten tens make one hundred.

00:00:47.421 --> 00:00:51.452 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Each place is ten times the place immediately to
its right.

00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:54.084 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Look at the seven in this number.

00:00:54.204 --> 00:00:57.411 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Before I explain, predict which place it holds.

00:00:57.531 --> 00:01:00.170 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Remember to count from ones at the right.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.600 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Look at four hundred and seventy two thousand,
six hundred and thirty one.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.422 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Put each digit into its own column, from hundred
thousands down to ones.

00:01:11.542 --> 00:01:14.537 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The seven is in the ten thousands column.

00:01:14.657 --> 00:01:18.524 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Its value is seventy thousand, not seven
thousand.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.757 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The two stands for two thousand, the six for six
hundred, and the three for thirty.

00:01:23.877 --> 00:01:30.228 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Reading the number in groups helps: four hundred
and seventy two thousand, then six hundred and
thirty one.

00:01:30.348 --> 00:01:32.466 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Every digit has a job.

00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:34.865 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Now look at these two fives.

00:01:34.985 --> 00:01:37.307 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Which one has the greater value?

00:01:37.427 --> 00:01:40.011 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Point to its column before we compare.

00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:45.177 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Now compare the two fives in fifty five
thousand.

00:01:45.297 --> 00:01:48.393 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The first five represents fifty thousand.

00:01:48.513 --> 00:01:51.383 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The second represents five thousand.

00:01:51.503 --> 00:01:56.812 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Fifty thousand is ten times five thousand, even
though the digits look the same.

00:01:57.167 --> 00:02:02.258 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
This relationship helps us multiply and divide
whole numbers by ten.

00:02:02.378 --> 00:02:05.854 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Thirty four times ten is three hundred and
forty.

00:02:05.974 --> 00:02:09.495 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Each digit takes a place worth ten times as
much.

00:02:09.615 --> 00:02:13.647 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Three hundred and forty divided by ten returns
thirty four.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.400 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A common mix-up is saying that the seven here is
just worth seven.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.342 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Seven names the digit, but its column tells us
its value.

00:02:22.462 --> 00:02:27.192 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
In the ten-thousands column, it represents seven
groups of ten thousand.

00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:33.085 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
In 583,214, what is the value of the eight?

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.755 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The eight is in the ten-thousands place.

00:02:40.875 --> 00:02:43.209 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Its value is eighty thousand.

00:02:43.567 --> 00:02:48.858 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Try the page example: five hundred and eighty
three thousand, two hundred and fourteen.

00:02:48.978 --> 00:02:53.811 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The eight is in the ten thousands place, so it
represents eighty thousand.

00:02:53.931 --> 00:02:57.498 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Remember: name the place first, then work out
the value.

00:02:57.618 --> 00:03:00.734 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One place left means ten times as much.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:06.364 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Our goal was to explain how a digit’s place
changes its value in a whole number.

00:03:06.484 --> 00:03:12.266 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You can now explain your strategy, check the
places carefully, and show why your answer makes
sense.

00:03:12.386 --> 00:03:16.001 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Choose a number of your own and explain it to
someone nearby.

00:03:16.121 --> 00:03:19.309 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Then try the lesson practice on Math Magic.
